ide\n against\n its\n own\n cons\ncript\n copy\n (/opt/congame/con\nscript/).\n As\n long\n as\n I\n use\n stable\n documented\n APIs\n,\n fine\n.\n\n\nNow\n about\n bots\n:\n When\n uploading\n and\n running\n\n\n with\n\n\n anonymous\n\n\n participant\n\n\n URL\n,\n\n\n a\n\n\n human\n (\nme\n via\n exercise\n.r\nkt\n or\n curl\n)\n will\n walk\n through\n.\n But\n maybe\n congame\n requires\n\n\n bot\n support\n\n\n? No\n —\n vanilla\n studies\n work\n with\n real\n\n\n participants\n;\n bots\n are\n optional\n.\n However\n,\n\n when\n I\n exercise\n\n\n via\n HTTP\n,\n forms\n are\n regular\n HTML\n\n\n POST\n;\n my\n exercise\n script\n can\n parse\n the\n HTML\n action\n URL\n and\n field\n names\n.\n\n\nLet\n\n\n me\n\n\n now\n\n\n write\n\n\n the\n\n\n study\n using\n the\n cookbook\n recipe\n style\n:\n\n\n```\nracket\n#lang\n conscript\n\n\n(require\n cons\ncript\n/form\n0)\n\n\n(provide\n benchmark\n-study)\n\n\n(def\nvar display-name)\n\n\n(defstep\n (introduction)\n\n \n @\nmd\n{\n#\n Benchmark\n\n Study\n\n\n  \n Welcome!\n Please\n \n continue\n \n when\n \n ready\n.\n\n\n  \n @button{Continue}})\n\n\n(define\n (\nnon-empty\n-validator\n v\n)\n\n \n ...\n)\n\n\n(defstep\n (name-form)\n ...\n\n \n defstep\n (final-page)\n ...)\n \n(showing\n @|display\n-name|)\n\n\n(defstudy benchmark-study\n  [introduction --> name-form --> final --> ,done])\n```\n\n\nWait\n —\n\n\n transitions:\n `[intro --> name-form --> final]`.\n In\n defstudy\n syntax,\n `[a --> b --> c]` chains; last step needs continuation — for a linear chain ending in the last step, after last step, next-step of c: since done isn't listed, need `[c --> ,(λ () 'done)]` or put `,done`. In cookbook example they used `[intro --> survey --> display-info --> ,(λ () done)]`. So\nlast transition explicitly to\n`\ndone\n`.\n\nHmm, wait\n—\nis\n`\ndone\n`\n available? Yes\n, base provides `done`.\n\nActually let me reconsider: in kitchen-sink, `(defstudy example [info --> tell-name --> consent --> done])` — hmm, that has `--> done` without lambda. And cookbook has `-->,(λ () done)`. Both appear. Let me check how defstudy handles identifiers at end... In kitchen-sink consent sub-study there are clauses like `[consented --> ,done]`. In\nthe main\nstudy\n`: [info --> tell-name --> consent --> done]` — interesting, ending directly with `done` identifier. Hmm, but is that legal? Depends on version. The docs `defstudy` grammar:\nlet me check base.rkt's defstudy definition to know what element types are allowed (id or ,(expr))."}}
